423 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Input Layer Data compression and input management leave systems exposed to prompt injection, adversarial deep learning, and data poisoning. Attackers can embed malicious payloads into input streams, bypass input filters, or exploit weaknesses in how compressed data is parsed. Recent research underscores the need for prompt canonicalisation, input sanitisation, and routine adversarial testing to prevent these threats \(NeuralTrust, 2025; OWASP, 2024\).)
/E (Input Layer Data compression and input management leave systems exposed to prompt injection, adversarial deep learning, and data poisoning. Attackers can embed malicious payloads into input streams, bypass input filters, or exploit weaknesses in how compressed data is parsed. Recent research underscores the need for prompt canonicalisation, input sanitisation, and routine adversarial testing to prevent these threats \(NeuralTrust, 2025; OWASP, 2024\).)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
424 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Model Layer Model compression techniques such as pruning, distillation, and quantisation can reduce robustness and privacy. Quantised and pruned models are more likely to suffer information leakage, membership inference attacks, and model inversion attempts, which could expose sensitive training data or proprietary algorithms \(BytePlus, 2025; Promwad, 2025\). Applying differential privacy and encryption is considered best practice for mitigating these risks \(BytePlus, 2025\).)
/E (Model Layer Model compression techniques such as pruning, distillation, and quantisation can reduce robustness and privacy. Quantised and pruned models are more likely to suffer information leakage, membership inference attacks, and model inversion attempts, which could expose sensitive training data or proprietary algorithms \(BytePlus, 2025; Promwad, 2025\). Applying differential privacy and encryption is considered best practice for mitigating these risks \(BytePlus, 2025\).)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [4]
>>
